// Stage-1 concept-art prompt for the E-09 image→3D pipeline. BAML owns the versioned
// TEXT prompt (rendered via b.request, the text extracted by the harness like the build/
// judge stages). The reference image (+ any prior render) are attached by the nano-banana
// client as multimodal inputs — the concept image itself is generated by Nano Banana
// (Gemini), so ClaudeStub here only shapes .request; b.parse is never called.

function FacadeConceptPrompt(design_doc: string, target_blocks: int, attached: string) -> string {
  client ClaudeStub
  prompt #"
    Generate CONCEPT ART of a Minecraft TEMPLE FACADE: a head-on FRONT ELEVATION — the one grand
    face — as a SINGLE ISOLATED STRUCTURE floating against a solid BLACK (#000000) background, so it
    segments cleanly for 3D reconstruction. No ground plane, no cast shadows, no sky, garden, water,
    plinth-on-ground, or surroundings of any kind. The whole facade is CENTERED and FULLY VISIBLE
    (not cropped — leave a small margin on every side), filling most of the frame, lit by bright,
    even STUDIO lighting with no dramatic shadows.

    Render it in authentic MINECRAFT BLOCK style — built from cubic blocks, with block-scale detail
    ONLY. The build is about {{ target_blocks }} blocks wide, so keep every feature BOLD and chunky.
    HARD LIMITS (anything finer will not survive voxelization, so do NOT draw it). These OVERRIDE
    the design document below: if it describes any of these, render the BOLD-block replacement
    instead, never the literal motif:
    - NO human, animal, or deity figures, NO soldiers, NO sculptural relief scenes or statues --
      EVEN IF the document places statues or figures in niches, panels, or recesses. Render every
      such niche or panel as a BLANK or bold ROSETTE panel, never a figure.
    - NO text, letters, numerals, calligraphy, or inscriptions of any kind -- and specifically NO
      nameplate, signboard, plaque, cartouche, or inscribed tablet above the entrance or anywhere
      else. Replace any such inscribed panel with a blank or bold rosette panel. Friezes and bands
      carry plain color-blocking or bold rosettes ONLY -- never a row of glyph-like square
      characters or symbol panels.
    - NO fine filigree, thin tracery, lacework, or sub-block ornament; NO star-burst or thin spiked
      finials. Cap towers, gables, and pinnacles with a SIMPLE chunky block, not a fine spike.
    Replace every such motif with BOLD GEOMETRIC block ornament instead — chunky panels, bands,
    stepped frames, rosettes, and large-scale COLOR BLOCKING. Each smallest feature should read as a
    few whole blocks. Massing, silhouette, and framed openings carry the design.

    Faithfully realize THIS finalized design document — its massing and proportion, its style and
    motifs, and above all its PALETTE (use its actual named colors; it is deliberately COLORFUL, never
    a white or monochrome wall). The background behind the structure must be SOLID #000000 black --
    NEVER white, light, grey, navy, or any coloured or gradient backdrop; the structure floats on pure
    black. So its edges read crisply against that black, keep the structure's OUTER SILHOUETTE in its
    brighter palette colors and do NOT place black, near-black, OR any dark or dim tone (e.g. dark or
    medium blue, deep purple) along the outline -- those merge into the black and break segmentation.
    In particular the OUTERMOST elements -- spires, finials, pinnacles, and the roof crest -- must be a
    bright, high-contrast palette color (e.g. gold or a warm light stone), never a dark or dim one, so
    the top edge cannot vanish into the black field. Reserve any dark tones for recessed interior
    areas only:

    {{ design_doc }}

    {{ attached }}
  "#
}

// Stage-1 concept-art prompt for the vConcept SCULPTURE mode (T-035-01 / E-13). Sibling of
// FacadeConceptPrompt, kept SEPARATE so the facade prompt stays frozen (its E-09 runs stay
// reproducible). Same transport: BAML renders the TEXT (b.parse never called for images), the
// harness attaches it to Nano Banana (Gemini), which generates the 3/4 object concept. Unlike
// the facade fn this frames a FREESTANDING OBJECT IN THE ROUND at a three-quarter view, not a
// head-on front elevation — the single image that grounds the 3-D build.
function SculptureConceptPrompt(design_doc: string, target_blocks: int, attached: string) -> string {
  client ClaudeStub
  prompt #"
    Generate CONCEPT ART of a Minecraft-block SCULPTURE: a SINGLE ISOLATED FREESTANDING OBJECT
    shown in a clear THREE-QUARTER (3/4) VIEW — angled so two sides and the top read at once,
    seen slightly from above, as if on a turntable — floating against a solid BLACK (#000000)
    background so it segments cleanly for 3D reconstruction. No ground plane, no cast shadows, no
    sky, pedestal-on-ground, scenery, or surroundings of any kind. The whole object is CENTERED
    and FULLY VISIBLE (not cropped — leave a small margin on every side), filling most of the
    frame, lit by bright, even STUDIO lighting with no dramatic shadows.

    Render it in authentic MINECRAFT BLOCK style — built from cubic blocks, with block-scale
    detail ONLY. The build is about {{ target_blocks }} blocks along its longest dimension, so keep
    every feature BOLD and chunky. HARD LIMITS (anything finer will not survive voxelization, so do
    NOT draw it). These OVERRIDE the design document below: if it describes any of these, render the
    BOLD-block replacement instead, never the literal motif:
    - NO sub-block ornament, fine filigree, thin tracery, lacework, hair strands, or thin spikes;
      every smallest feature must read as a few WHOLE blocks. Approximate curves and round forms with
      stepped, faceted blocks, never smooth surfaces.
    - NO text, letters, numerals, or inscriptions of any kind.
    Replace every such motif with BOLD GEOMETRIC block forms — chunky masses, stepped facets, and
    large-scale COLOR BLOCKING. Massing, silhouette, and proportion carry the design.

    Faithfully realize THIS finalized design document — its essential masses and proportion, its
    motifs, and above all its PALETTE (use its actual named colors; commit to its dominant /
    supporting / accent hierarchy, never a flat grey or monochrome blob). The object is a SOLID
    sculpture IN THE ROUND with real depth and roundness on every visible side — not a flat relief
    or a wall. The background behind the object must be SOLID #000000 black -- NEVER white, light,
    grey, or any coloured or gradient backdrop; the object floats on pure black. So its edges read
    crisply against that black, keep the object's OUTER SILHOUETTE in its brighter palette colors and
    do NOT place black, near-black, OR any dark or dim tone along the outline -- those merge into the
    black and break segmentation. Reserve any dark tones for recessed or interior areas only:

    {{ design_doc }}

    {{ attached }}
  "#
}

// Stage-1 concept-art prompt — vConcept SCULPTURE, PALETTE-AWARE .v2 (T-040-01 / S-040 / E-14).
// Sibling of SculptureConceptPrompt, kept SEPARATE so the .v1 prompt stays frozen (its E-13 runs
// stay reproducible). Adds exactly ONE thing over .v1: it consumes a VALUE-TRUE palette legend and
// is told to match the ATTACHED SWATCH GRID's real block VALUES (often darker than the block's name
// implies), closing the value-drift gap (the concept blocked hue but not value, so the render later
// surprised with a darker block). The swatch grid IMAGE is attached by the harness via the same
// multimodal path any reference image uses; BAML renders only the TEXT. The .v1 body below is copied
// character-for-character from SculptureConceptPrompt; only the VALUE-TRUE PALETTE block is new.
function SculptureConceptPromptV2(design_doc: string, target_blocks: int, palette_swatches: string, attached: string) -> string {
  client ClaudeStub
  prompt #"
    Generate CONCEPT ART of a Minecraft-block SCULPTURE: a SINGLE ISOLATED FREESTANDING OBJECT
    shown in a clear THREE-QUARTER (3/4) VIEW — angled so two sides and the top read at once,
    seen slightly from above, as if on a turntable — floating against a solid BLACK (#000000)
    background so it segments cleanly for 3D reconstruction. No ground plane, no cast shadows, no
    sky, pedestal-on-ground, scenery, or surroundings of any kind. The whole object is CENTERED
    and FULLY VISIBLE (not cropped — leave a small margin on every side), filling most of the
    frame, lit by bright, even STUDIO lighting with no dramatic shadows.

    Render it in authentic MINECRAFT BLOCK style — built from cubic blocks, with block-scale
    detail ONLY. The build is about {{ target_blocks }} blocks along its longest dimension, so keep
    every feature BOLD and chunky. HARD LIMITS (anything finer will not survive voxelization, so do
    NOT draw it). These OVERRIDE the design document below: if it describes any of these, render the
    BOLD-block replacement instead, never the literal motif:
    - NO sub-block ornament, fine filigree, thin tracery, lacework, hair strands, or thin spikes;
      every smallest feature must read as a few WHOLE blocks. Approximate curves and round forms with
      stepped, faceted blocks, never smooth surfaces.
    - NO text, letters, numerals, or inscriptions of any kind.
    Replace every such motif with BOLD GEOMETRIC block forms — chunky masses, stepped facets, and
    large-scale COLOR BLOCKING. Massing, silhouette, and proportion carry the design.

    Faithfully realize THIS finalized design document — its essential masses and proportion, its
    motifs, and above all its PALETTE (use its actual named colors; commit to its dominant /
    supporting / accent hierarchy, never a flat grey or monochrome blob). The object is a SOLID
    sculpture IN THE ROUND with real depth and roundness on every visible side — not a flat relief
    or a wall. The background behind the object must be SOLID #000000 black -- NEVER white, light,
    grey, or any coloured or gradient backdrop; the object floats on pure black. So its edges read
    crisply against that black, keep the object's OUTER SILHOUETTE in its brighter palette colors and
    do NOT place black, near-black, OR any dark or dim tone along the outline -- those merge into the
    black and break segmentation. Reserve any dark tones for recessed or interior areas only:

    VALUE-TRUE PALETTE (this is the .v2 addition): An exact SWATCH GRID of the REAL Minecraft blocks
    for this build is attached as an image. Each swatch is that block's TRUE in-render color and
    VALUE — frequently DARKER than the block's name suggests. Match every surface to these real
    swatch VALUES, not to an imagined or idealized hue: if a block's true swatch is dark, paint that
    surface dark. Where the design document names a block, use that block's swatch VALUE for it. This
    overrides any lighter impression the block's NAME might give — but it does NOT override the
    silhouette rule above: keep the brightest palette blocks on the outer edge and spend the darker
    true-value blocks on interior / recessed surfaces. The legend maps each named block to its true
    value (L* lightness, 0 = black .. 100 = white):
    {{ palette_swatches }}

    {{ design_doc }}

    {{ attached }}
  "#
}
